ums and from very  unusual places to sort of break away  from the equivalents quality equals  money experts and especially we  journalists expect from from a biannual  that it kind of reflects the  contemporary art world and also the  tight geist the in a way the state of  the world is that something a Biennale  can really offer well I wanted to call  the benighted encyclopedia palace  because the expectation of bionis to  capture all the world's knowledge is  certainly doomed to fail as much as the  or it is dream I do think the thing  today being contemporary also means  having a access to the past and to the  present that is completely different  this show by looking at the desire to  know and see everything talks about a  present that is obsessed with  information that is obsessed with  knowledge I'd like to say anyway it's a  prehistory of an age of information or a  prehistory or Wikipedia and it tries to  look at how in the last century we have  lived with an obsession of knowledge and  information so I hope you will talk  about the present but from a perspective  that connects to the past the idea of  national pavilions because we don't have  that art show the answer encyclopedic  palace but we also have almost 90  national pavilions and the number is  growing by the year the idea was always  contested but that doesn't seem very  relevant now that obviously the  participation in Venice seems to be more  and more attractive why is it so well I  often make a joke that the National  pavilion are a bit like the atomic bomb  as long as the superpowers had it he was  fine and then when others people wanted  the superpowers say oh that's obsolete I  think it's the fact that you have 88  nations that come to Venice and they  want to express their existence on the  world stage means that the model is far  from being obsolete it means also that  the Biennal has been able to transform  itself if you  the Giardini you have a picture of the  world that it's very much based on an  old-fashioned Western centric sense of  the world and now in the arson alley we  have Chinese pavilion we have  Argentinean pavilion we have a war that  is in the United Emirates we in the  Arsenal I think it's a new geopolitical  landscape that has been presented and  the other day I just looked at the map  of the world in the office where we put  little flags on all the countries that  are represented and 88 countries is  still less than half of the countries in  the world so I think it's important that  we keep present in art from everywhere  in the world and to use this as an  occasion to present a word that it's  international but not flattened and I  think that's what Venice is very special  for because it's a global picture with  lots of contrast and not a flat world  the new centers of the art world like  Latin America even Africa the Arab world  are they properly represented when I  listen to you it sounds like there  arrived in Venice they're here and there  is no more discussion about it yeah I  think Venice is a very special place  it's a place where the West is dreamt of  the East back from the days of Marco  Polo and certainly it is a place where  many countries who have come to the  attention of the West recently a founder  place and I think this year we have in  the same building in India sonali we  have Argentina United Emirates Turkey  and also the Vatican the Holy See and  they have all opened permanent pavilions  and I think that's an exciting view of  what the world can be today you have  written that you want to show you want  to explore and at the Biennale  especially in your show the power of the  imagination now in a world that is  flooded by images day by day in a world  of information what is the contribution  of the artist  I mean he has to compete with all the  images we see every day I wanted in fact  the exhibition is very dense the in the  central pavilion and in their Sonali  they are more than 4,000  individual objects I wanted to make a  show that anyway can compete on the same  level as the media by flooding the the  eyes of the viewer but at the same time  I hope that each and every image in the  show as an intensity and a purity that  escapes that the volatility of  commercial images hands melting with an  art historian there was a great  inspiration for this show said that the  first medium of images are not the TV  the camera or the screen but is actually  our body and our brain when we close our  eyes with dreams and we see images and I  hope the exhibition can help the viewer  focus on the internal images and the  space of imagination and what we see  when we move away from technology or  when we discover that we are self media  and we ourself carry images within our  heads have you integrated the digital  the virtual world in your show the show  in a way looks at the prehistory of our  society and particularly in the arsenal  the Arsenal is structure following the  progression of 16th century wonder  camera and in cabinets of curiosity  which were usually organized as a  progression from the natural to the  artificial so the show starts with the  artist looking at nature trying to  decrypt the code of nature any answer  with the series of works mostly by  younger artists that represent our  digital condition and this idea of a  universal visibility and and so yes I  think the the end of the show is very  much about this illusion we have that we  can see everything and that everything  is in our pocket through the help of  digital technology so it seems like  we're getting a long stretch from the  past to the future and everything  included in that palace I don't know if  that's a mirage you work in New York as  a museum director you've co-managed  they're very successful Berlin be  another some years ago now you're in  Venice what's the most inspiring place  for you you're a you know like a nomad  of the art world so  I don't think of myself much of a nomad  I say as a joke that it's important to  spend as much time on books than on  planes and and this show anyway is also  tribute to the book starts with Young's  red book and he talks about books as  places for the imagination and places  where you can hide and where you can  escape at the same time I work very well  both in New York because it's a very  exciting place and I feel all surrounded  to serve the foundation in Milan where  we do know magic projects I think  ultimately and this is what this  exhibition is also about I work very  well in my head it's yeah I tried also  for this show not to travel everywhere  in the world  the but also to travel across history  and and to remind ourself that new  discoveries can be made when you explore  less canonical works thank you for the  interview and good luck for the show  thank you
 

